  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 310 AM Saturday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Isolated pockets of heavy rainfall are possible \nonce again this afternoon into early evening, mainly over the S and \nE. Drier weather starts Sun and lasts through much of next week.\n\nA diffuse surface boundary remains over the region, running NE to SW \nthrough central NC, with dewpoints mostly in the 60s to its NW and \n70-75 to its SE. In the mid levels, a baggy shear axis to our W has \nbeen steadily drifting ESE toward our area while weakening, and \nearly this morning this shear axis extends through the NC Foothills, \nkeeping us on the weakly perturbed SSW mid level flow on its east \nside. PW values were around 110%-125% of normal NW-SE Fri but are \nprojected to decrease to 90%-110% of normal NW-SE today, as both the \nsurface boundary and the stream of deeper moisture shift gradually \nto our SE, and as the shear axis weakens further while maintaining \nan ESE drift. Observational trends and the latest model and ensemble \noutput still show sctd showers and storms developing today, \nprimarily S and E of the Triad, fueled in part by passing weak mid \nlevel perturbations yielding modest DPVA, upper divergence in the \nright rear jet quad, and SBCAPE peaking around 1000-1500 J/kg this \nafternoon. Drier air in our NW Piedmont and lesser dynamic \ncontribution to lift will mean lower coverage there, perhaps just \nisolated at best. The weakening deep layer bulk shear and modest \nCAPE should keep the severe threat low today, but the continued slow \ncell motion will bring about another risk for locally excessive \nrainfall, primarily across our S and E where the PW will be greater. \nMost of the forecast area is under a Marginal risk (level 1 of 4) \nfor excessive rainfall (excluding the Triad) Saturday. Convective \nactivity should dwindle with loss of heating by mid evening, leading \nto fair to partly cloudy skies overnight.\n\nAs the shear axis weakens further and settles to our SSE with strong \nridging building in from the W, PW will plummet further, with a loss \nof low level access to Gulf and Atlantic moisture as surface ridging \nspreads E-W to our S. As a result, rain chances will become quite \nlow Sun through at least Wed, with building heat. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... A hot start to September is expected across central \nNC. \n\nAfter one near to slightly cooler than normal day today (highs in \nthe lower-to-mid-80s), a warming trend will commence on Sunday that \ncontinues into late next week. This is due in large part to a strong \n~595 dam mid-level ridge initially centered over the Southern Plains \nthat shifts east into the lower MS and TN Valleys and eventually the \nCarolinas. At the surface, a Bermuda High will bring warm SW flow to \nthe region through the period. With mostly sunny skies and very low \n(if any) precipitation chances, excessive heat will be a concern \nacross central NC as we start the workweek and especially by mid to \nlate week. \n\nBy Monday, forecast highs are in the lower-to-mid-90s, with mid-to-\nupper-90s by Tuesday that last into Friday. The hottest days look to \nbe Wednesday and Thursday when isolated 100F readings can't be ruled \nout. The one silver lining is dew points don't look overly \noppressive through the period and are forecast to mix out to the \nupper-60s to lower-70s each afternoon (other than perhaps the far \nSE). Still, with the exception of the far northern and northwestern \nPiedmont, this is enough for heat indices in the lower-to-mid-100s \nfrom Tuesday through Friday. The WPC Experimental HeatRisk is in the \nMajor to Extreme categories for much of the region during this \nperiod. This means the heat is expected to affect anyone without \nadequate cooling/hydration. Heat Advisories may eventually be needed \nfor a portion of the area, especially given the expected prolonged \nnature of this heat and overnight lows only dropping into the lower-\nto-mid-70s.",
